## Detailed Decision Records

### D01 — Drivetrain Ratio: Speed vs Torque

**Problem:**  
The earlier drivetrain used gearing intended to increase vehicle speed.

**Previous approach:**  
Approximately **1.5× speed increase** through the drivetrain.

**Observed limitation:**  
Increasing output speed reduced available wheel torque and increased drivetrain load. Under demanding conditions, this reduced the useful operating margin of the motor.

**Alternatives considered:**

1. retain the speed-increasing gearing;
2. reduce the ratio partially;
3. use direct **1:1** transmission;
4. replace the motor while retaining aggressive gearing.

**Decision:**  
Use a **1:1 drivetrain ratio** together with the revised motor configuration.

**Trade-off:**

| Advantage | Cost |
| --- | --- |
| Higher usable torque | Lower theoretical maximum speed |
| Lower drivetrain stress | Less aggressive straight-line acceleration |
| Greater reliability margin | Requires speed to come primarily from motor selection |
| Simpler power transmission | Gives up an attractive but unnecessary headline speed |

**Reasoning:**  
The competition rewards completed, controlled runs rather than unloaded drivetrain speed. Reliability and controllability therefore had greater engineering value than maximum theoretical velocity.

