### D22 — Post-Nationals Chassis Redesign

The post-Nationals redesign was not performed simply to produce a different-looking robot.

The mechanical layout constrained:

- camera location;
- camera viewing angle;
- usable field of view;
- ToF mounting;
- wiring;
- obstacle clearance;
- serviceability.

The final design therefore changed the chassis and mounting architecture to support the desired sensing system.

This demonstrates an important design dependency:

```text
Perception requirement
        ↓
Sensor geometry
        ↓
Mount geometry
        ↓
Chassis requirement
        ↓
Mechanical redesign
```

Software requirements therefore directly influenced the mechanical architecture.

---

### D25 — Modular Software Architecture

The robot uses separate modules for functions such as:

```text
drive
encoder
vision
heading
ToF sensing
parking
challenge control
```

instead of placing every operation inside one large script.

**Reasoning:**

If a complete run fails, a monolithic architecture makes it difficult to determine whether the problem originated from:

- motor control;
- steering;
- sensing;
- perception;
- heading;
- parking logic;
- high-level state control.

With modular software, individual components can be tested before integration.

```text
Component test
      ↓
Verify subsystem
      ↓
Integrate
      ↓
Challenge test
```

The trade-off is additional interface management between modules, but the improvement in debugging and reuse justified that complexity.

